// EXPECT_COMPILE_ERROR E_SLICE_ROLE_UNSATISFIED
//
// Registry 221.1 #1022 neg, and this is the carrier the row's caveat demands.
// The owner found the class by reading the spec against the code: D470 declares
// the slice role by SIGNATURES — `@index(r Range) -> V` plus
// `@end_index() -> int` — while both places that asked about it asked by method
// NAME. `IntBag` below is exactly the shape that slips through a name check: it
// declares `index` and it declares `end_index`, so by name the role looks
// satisfied, but its only `index` overload takes an `int`. A range slice has
// nothing to call.
//
// What the name check produced before the fix, and why it was expensive: the
// type passed, codegen synthesized a call carrying a `Range`, and the failure
// surfaced as an overload-resolution error — someone else's message, pointing
// away from the cause. The refusal must name the ROLE and the missing overload
// instead.
//
// The measured reason a name cannot decide this: one type carries BOTH
// overloads under one name. In emitted C they stand side by side as
// `Nova_Vec_method_index(Nova_Vec* nova_self, nova_int i)` and the
// `NovaValue_Range` one, so "declares index" says nothing about slicing.
//
// The positive counterpart is not written here on purpose — it already exists
// twice, in `openrange_len_field_heap.nv` and `openrange_len_field_value.nv`,
// where a user type carries `@index(r Range)` AND `@end_index()` and slices in
// both storage classes.

module neg.slice_role_int_index_only_neg

type IntBag {
    len int
}

// The only `index` overload takes an int, not a Range. By NAME this is
// indistinguishable from a slicing type; by SIGNATURE it is not.
fn IntBag @index(i int) -> int => i

// Present on purpose: `end_index` exists, so a check that merely looks for the
// two names finds both and concludes the role is satisfied.
fn IntBag @end_index() -> int => @len

fn main() -> () {
    ro b = IntBag { len: 10 }
    // nova:expect E_SLICE_ROLE_UNSATISFIED -- `index` exists, no overload takes a `Range`
    ro v = b[1..3]
    ro _ = v
}
